# demo: performance and cost report

What does adopting this package add to latency, CPU, memory and compute spend -- and how does that grow with workload? Three views, from the whole to the part: **(1)** the end-to-end total, **(2)** every function on its own, **(3)** which functions make up the total. New to benchmarks? Read [READING-BENCHMARKS.md](READING-BENCHMARKS.md) first.

## What adopting this package costs

For a typical workload of **80 items** per operation, routing the work through `demo` adds **1.00 ms** per operation compared with a bare-minimum baseline (**100%** of the baseline). Overall, it grows O(1) with workload size (measured exponent 0.00). At list prices that is on the order of **~$0.001 – $0.01 per million operations** of compute.

> The relative figure and the growth class are the dependable ones: both come from the same run, so they survive a change of machine. Dollar figures are **order-of-magnitude** estimates from published list prices on shared hardware (see _Cost model_ below) -- good for comparing one package with another, not for budgeting.

| Cost | Typical (80 items) | Largest (320 items) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Added latency per operation | 1.00 ms | 1.00 ms |
| Added latency, relative to baseline | 100% | 100% |
| Added CPU time per operation | 500 µs | 500 µs |
| Added memory per operation (heap delta) | 0 B | 0 B |
| Compute cost per 1M operations (order of magnitude) | ~$0.001 – $0.01 | ~$0.001 – $0.01 |
| Single-core throughput ceiling of the overhead alone | 1,000 ops/s | 1,000 ops/s |

## 1. End-to-end: the package's total impact

Measures what routing the workload through the package adds.

- **Baseline (no package):** Process items directly.
- **With the package:** Process items via the package.

Both sides use empty or minimal functions on purpose, so the difference is the package's own cost -- not the cost of the work an application would plug into it. Real applications add their own work on top; this is the floor the package imposes.

**Variables that could change this result**

| Variable | How it is handled | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| items | swept | The tier axis. |
| mode | fixed at "strict" | Validation mode. |

The baseline is an empty or minimal function, so it costs almost nothing and the _relative_ overhead can look enormous (shown as a multiple of the baseline). Read the absolute columns -- time, CPU and dollars added -- they are what a bill and a latency budget are made of.

| items | Baseline | With package | Added | Added vs baseline | Added CPU | Est. $ / 1M ops |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 20 | 1.00 ms | 2.00 ms | 1.00 ms | 100% | 500 µs | $0.0021 – $0.0056 |
| 40 | 1.00 ms | 2.00 ms | 1.00 ms | 100% | 500 µs | $0.0021 – $0.0056 |
| 80 | 1.00 ms | 2.00 ms | 1.00 ms | 100% | 500 µs | $0.0021 – $0.0056 |
| 160 | 1.00 ms | 2.00 ms | 1.00 ms | 100% | 500 µs | $0.0021 – $0.0056 |
| 320 | 1.00 ms | 2.00 ms | 1.00 ms | 100% | 500 µs | $0.0021 – $0.0056 |

**How the total grows:** O(1) (constant), exponent 0.00 over 5 sizes.

## 2. Function by function

Every function the package exposes is measured on its own across the full size ladder, then its measured growth rate is compared with the big-O we documented for it. A function whose measured class **differs** from its documented one is the most useful thing in this report: either the documentation or the code is wrong.

| Function | Documented | Measured | Agreement | At 80 | At 320 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `scan` (all) | O(n) | O(n) | ✅ matches | 8.00 ms | 16.0 ms (at 160) |
| `scan` (one) | O(1) | n/a | ❔ not enough data | 1.00 ms (at 20) | 1.00 ms (at 20) |
| `plain` | O(1) | n/a | ❔ not enough data | n/a | n/a |

### `scan`

**Why we benchmark it.** Scans the whole set once.

**What poor performance would mean.** Scans dominate every operation.

**Expected growth: O(n).** A sufficiently long and honest explanation.

**Measured on a shorter ladder (20, 40, 80, 160).** Larger sizes take minutes and add no information.

**Variables that could change its cost**

| Variable | How it is handled | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| items | swept | The tier axis. |

**Deliberately not covered**

- **disk cache** -- Needs a real filesystem.

#### Variant `all`

Everything is scanned.

**Measured: O(n)** (exponent 1.00, 4 sizes) -- ✅ matches.

| items | Median | p95 | CPU (median) | Heap Δ | Ops/s |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 20 | 2.00 ms | 2.40 ms | 1.00 ms | 200 B | 500 |
| 40 | 4.00 ms | 4.80 ms | 2.00 ms | 400 B | 250 |
| 80 | 8.00 ms | 9.60 ms | 4.00 ms | 800 B | 125 |
| 160 | 16.0 ms | 19.2 ms | 8.00 ms | 1.6 KiB | 63 |

#### Variant `one`

A single item is scanned.

**Expected for this variant: O(1).** One item does not depend on n.

| items | Median | p95 | CPU (median) | Heap Δ | Ops/s |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 20 | 1.00 ms | 1.20 ms | 500 µs | 200 B | n/a |
| 40 | ❌ failed |  |  |  |  |
| 80 | ❌ failed |  |  |  |  |

### `plain`

**Why we benchmark it.** A function that is never part of the end-to-end run.

**What poor performance would mean.** Nothing user-visible.

**Expected growth: O(1).** A fixed number of steps.

**Variables that could change its cost**

| Variable | How it is handled | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| keys | fixed at 3 | Three keys, always. |

## 3. What makes up one end-to-end operation

_No function declared how it is used in the end-to-end run (`inEndToEnd`), so there is nothing to attribute._

